--- narrowed/guide-hal.txt
+++ bootstrap-reuse/guide-hal.txt
@@ -1,9 +1,9 @@
 Use dotnet-inspect to answer from inspected evidence. In an installed environment, acquire/run with `dnx dotnet-inspect -y -- <arguments>`; this trial's recorder runs a source-pinned executable. Do not install or restore a project.
 Orient by command: `package query` searches packages; `find` locates API symbols; `type` and `member` inspect APIs; `extensions` finds extension methods; `depends` inspects dependencies. Package IDs/versions, type names, member signatures and vocabulary value IDs are different identities. Copy identities from results; do not infer an identity from a display label.
-Use discover -> explain -> inspect when task knowledge is missing. A known command's `--help` supplies parser options. Ordinary `-Q` describes query-capable sections and named facets without executing an inspection. Root `explain "search text" --json` discovers installed resources; copy returned paths. Text/help, ordinary inspection JSON and capability discovery do not need a reading projection.
+Use discover -> explain -> inspect when task knowledge is missing. A known command's `--help` supplies parser options. Ordinary `-Q` describes query-capable sections and named facets without executing an inspection. Root `explain "search text" --json` discovers available capabilities; copy returned paths. For vocabulary roots, use `explain vocabularies --json` and copy the returned canonical path instead of repeatedly searching for value names. Text/help, ordinary inspection JSON and capability discovery do not need a reading projection.
 Member `--explain` supplies text command or resolved-subject explanation; do not combine that primary output with `--json`. Where advertised, `-E .tips` appends related gestures on stderr after an inspection. Companion guidance is useful only for a missing task fact; do not append it merely to exercise the feature.
 For query-space, query-facet and vocabulary reading use `explain <path> .hal --json`. Package Query also admits acquisition-free `package query --explain` (HAL JSON); `--explain -Q <facet-key[,facet-key]>` narrows installed facets and includes their required context and CLI bindings. It accepts no package seed or execution predicates. Other command families may have different support; check help.
-Both reading representations are self-contained. `scope` qualifies completeness for the selected population, not the whole catalog. For an exact vocabulary root, `-Q <native-value-id[,native-value-id]>` narrows either reading projection before serialization; IDs are case-sensitive. This is a free narrowing operation, then use your own jq. It is separate from `-n` result limits and `--depth` graph expansion. Request omitted values only if needed to answer the task; omission from a selected population does not establish that a value is invalid.
+Both reading representations are self-contained and carry equivalent domain facts. Switching between direct and HAL cannot add missing definitions or identity details. `scope` qualifies completeness for the selected population, not the whole catalog. For an exact vocabulary root, `-Q <native-value-id[,native-value-id]>` narrows either reading projection before serialization; IDs are case-sensitive. This is a free narrowing operation, then use your own jq. It is separate from `-n` result limits and `--depth` graph expansion. Request omitted values only if needed to answer the task; omission from a selected population does not establish that a value is invalid.
 HAL uses `_embedded` for locally included data and `_links` for navigation. Pass a returned `inspect-resource:` href unchanged to `explain --json`; it resolves locally, not through HTTP. Query/facet and vocabulary reading have focused projections; do not use the full `.contract` unless declaration details are needed.
-Save responses and read with your own jq. Keep results, diagnostics and scope/limit qualifications; an envelope's Content holds results while Share and diagnostics carry other evidence. Ordinary JSON is result data and is not necessarily HAL. Read text for help or textual explanation.
+Save responses and read with your own jq. Use `-Q` on a needed first read when the native keys are known. If a complete response already contains the needed facts, narrow that cached response with jq; do not request a narrower serialized copy. Keep results, diagnostics and scope/limit qualifications; an envelope's Content holds results while Share and diagnostics carry other evidence. Ordinary JSON is result data and is not necessarily HAL. Read text for help or textual explanation.
 Answer from current and locally included data first. Follow a relation or request another resource only for a specific missing fact needed for the task. The user values low latency; verification-only requests are not permitted. Execute needed inspections and stop when their evidence answers the question.
